00:03:41.219 --> 00:03:42.260
FELIPE HOFFA: Yes, it is.

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:44.450
So one of the examples
you have on the book

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:47.660
is how to look at
similar artists

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.670
based on Wikipedia data.

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:50.780
How are you doing that?

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:52.863
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT:
Yes, so generally, the idea

00:03:52.863 --> 00:03:56.980
was-- if you look at Wikipedia
that generally used to extract

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:58.591
information about the content.

00:03:58.591 --> 00:04:00.090
Like you have a
page about an artist

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:02.690
and from there you can
have the genre to play,

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:04.490
you can have
influences, and so on.

00:04:04.490 --> 00:04:06.600
So they're going to
do something different

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:09.690
and look at how people
edit Wikipedia pages,

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:12.450
and see from those edits if
you can infer correlations.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:15.554
So if you use Amazon, if
you buy stuff on Amazon,

00:04:15.554 --> 00:04:16.470
you have these things.

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:18.160
People who bought
that also bought this.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:21.579
And the idea was to do something
similar with Wikipedia did.

00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:24.190
So people who edit this
page, also edit that one.

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:26.980
And see if you can find relation
if you're an artist based

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:28.550
on the way people read pages.

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:29.383
FELIPE HOFFA: I see.

00:04:29.383 --> 00:04:31.730
So you're looking at people
that edited this page also

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:33.120
edited this other artist.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:33.710
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: Exactly.

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:36.010
FELIPE HOFFA: So can we
take a look at this example?

00:04:36.010 --> 00:04:36.320
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: Sure.

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:36.910
Yes.

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:40.020
So for that, I'm using
the public Wikipedia edits

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:41.780
data sets which
is one of the data

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:44.201
sets you're sharing with
the-- with BigQuery.

00:04:44.201 --> 00:04:45.200
That's the big data set.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:48.055
So anyone can run a
similar experiment.

00:04:48.055 --> 00:04:49.430
And if you look
at the data sets,

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:52.520
you'll see that for
every Wikipedia edit,

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:55.380
so we have the title
and the ID of the page.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:57.020
And you have also
the contributor ID.

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:59.670
So that's what we need
to run that [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:02.080
because when you have
this information,

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:05.280
you can find that people who
edited a particular page also

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:08.442
edited another one, and
that's how we-- we're

00:05:08.442 --> 00:05:09.650
building that first use case.

00:05:09.650 --> 00:05:12.050
So let me get the
query from there.

00:05:12.050 --> 00:05:14.055
And go in detail.

00:05:14.055 --> 00:05:15.680
FELIPE HOFFA: How
big is this data set?

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:18.941
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: The
data set is 300 million rows,

00:05:18.941 --> 00:05:19.440
I think.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:20.810
FELIPE HOFFA: Excellent.

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:22.557
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: So

00:05:22.557 --> 00:05:23.890
FELIPE HOFFA: Pass a query here?

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:25.640
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT:
Run the query here.

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:28.660
So what that query does
there, I took the page

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:31.709
of the clash, the vendor-- and

00:05:31.709 --> 00:05:33.500
FELIPE HOFFA: So the
clash is number 3043--

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:34.170
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT:
Yeah so that's

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:35.586
the idea of the
page in Wikipedia.

00:05:35.586 --> 00:05:39.150
So every page got an ID, and the
ID won't change in real time.

00:05:39.150 --> 00:05:41.487
So you can use the ID to
make those kind of queries.

00:05:41.487 --> 00:05:45.660
So, that subquery there
will find all the people

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:48.310
that contributed to that page.

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:50.730
I am excluding the
bots there, because we

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:52.610
want to get real edits.

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:54.990
And I am also excluding
minor contributions

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:59.200
which can be just people
fixing a comma or typo.

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:02.540
And there from that
list, I'm getting simply

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:05.417
all the other pages that
those people have edited.

00:06:05.417 --> 00:06:07.500
FELIPE HOFFA: And counting--
and ranking them by--

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:09.025
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: Ranking
them by the number of edits.

00:06:09.025 --> 00:06:11.470
So, lets run that
and see what we have.

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:14.560
So that query is very fast.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:15.680
It was in the cache.

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:18.260
But we see that there
was other query,

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:19.910
text only five or
six [INAUDIBLE]

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:21.964
two pairs this
300 million words.

00:06:21.964 --> 00:06:23.505
FELIPE HOFFA: So in
the results here,

00:06:23.505 --> 00:06:25.110
we're getting
[INAUDIBLE] Hamilton.

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:28.329
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: Yeah,
so we have very strange things,

00:06:28.329 --> 00:06:30.120
cause in a way, they're
kind of mainstream.

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:33.110
So you see that people also
edit pages that have nothing

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:36.020
