00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:30.896
But that's not
with the playlists.

00:08:30.896 --> 00:08:34.850
So what I've done there was
to get from that API 500,000

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:39.544
playlists, and then
put that into BigQuery.

00:08:39.544 --> 00:08:40.669
And start running analysis.

00:08:40.669 --> 00:08:43.909
So one of the nice
things there-- and that's

00:08:43.909 --> 00:08:45.450
also a nice thing
about BigQuery when

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:47.950
you want to run ETL
pipeline or data

00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:51.340
analysis, is that you can
directly push Gs on data

00:08:51.340 --> 00:08:54.920
into BigQuery, as
long as [INAUDIBLE]

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:56.860
So you don't have
to do it inETL.

00:08:56.860 --> 00:08:58.990
You just put the Gs
on like in that case,

00:08:58.990 --> 00:09:01.220
like the one that was
provided by Geezer.

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:03.170
And just try to scheme
out, let's say, well,

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:06.080
in those-- in those
files we love.

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:07.750
We have an artist.

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:10.990
And the playlist will have
a track, on multiple tracks,

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:13.590
and every track will have
an artist, and so on.

00:09:13.590 --> 00:09:15.590
That's-- you just
define the Gs on schema,

00:09:15.590 --> 00:09:19.015
and give that to BigQuery, and
they will correct the tables

00:09:19.015 --> 00:09:20.499
and all the content for you.

00:09:20.499 --> 00:09:23.040
FELIPE HOFFA: So you don't know
that half a million playlists

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:25.696
from Deezer to this JSON
data directly into BigQuery.

00:09:25.696 --> 00:09:26.820
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: Yes.

