00:09:30.570 --> 00:09:33.490
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: So we
look-- we have the data there.

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:38.720
So, that's for a playlist, so
a playlist got a rating and ID.

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:40.977
You can have things like
title duration and so on.

00:09:40.977 --> 00:09:42.560
And we'll see that
for every playlist,

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:43.690
we get a number of tracks.

00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:46.120
So those are recurs,
that can be repeated.

00:09:46.120 --> 00:09:48.750
And a track got some artists.

00:09:48.750 --> 00:09:50.695
Every artist got an
ID and then the track

00:09:50.695 --> 00:09:53.290
also got an album ID
and so on, and so on.

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:56.220
So what's interesting
is that that directly

00:09:56.220 --> 00:09:58.440
comes from the Deezer API.

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:00.490
So let me show you.

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:03.004
If you look at the--

00:10:03.004 --> 00:10:04.420
FELIPE HOFFA: These
are playlists?

00:10:04.420 --> 00:10:07.890
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT:
Original thing there.

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:10.220
So that's the Gs on
that I used to translate

00:10:10.220 --> 00:10:12.960
the Gs on that comes
from that, from Geezer

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:14.680
directly to BigQuery.

00:10:14.680 --> 00:10:18.170
So instead of running an
extraction on every result

00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:21.050
I got from the API to
transfer-- transform their Gson

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:25.570
into a sequence to push
that in your data base.

00:10:25.570 --> 00:10:27.820
You just directly pass the
Gs on with that kind of--

00:10:27.820 --> 00:10:29.170
FELIPE HOFFA: Using that schema?

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:29.670
DR. ALEXANDRE
PASSANT: So the schema

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:31.560
says what we've seen
in the database.

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:34.340
We have a rating and ID, and
then we have some tracks,

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:37.200
and each track is a
recur that contains

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:41.610
a repeated number of artists,
and every artist also--

00:10:41.610 --> 00:10:43.140
or every track of on an album.

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:44.692
And as a single
title, and so on.

00:10:44.692 --> 00:10:46.900
FELIPE HOFFA: And what can
you do with this data, now

00:10:46.900 --> 00:10:47.590
that you have--

00:10:47.590 --> 00:10:47.980
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: Yes.

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:50.800
FELIPE HOFFA: Half a million
playlists from a lot of people.

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:51.760
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: So,
you can do lots of things.

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:54.810
So the obvious one is just if
you for instance want to find

00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:57.590
what's popular, or what's--
what's most popular.

00:10:57.590 --> 00:10:59.960
You can just simple--
very simple com queries.

00:10:59.960 --> 00:11:02.456
So that's simple,
that's not very fun,

00:11:02.456 --> 00:11:04.900
because there's
no real analytics.

00:11:04.900 --> 00:11:08.080
But what you can do is use a
similar approach to what we've

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:10.910
just seen with
Wikipedia, is to find

00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:12.130
similarities between artists.

00:11:12.130 --> 00:11:15.830
So people that use that
artist in a playlist, what are

00:11:15.830 --> 00:11:17.930
the other artist that they use?

00:11:17.930 --> 00:11:21.130
In a sense, that means that
if they put artists together,

00:11:21.130 --> 00:11:24.099
there's probably a similarity
at least for people, obviously.

00:11:24.099 --> 00:11:25.640
FELIPE HOFFA: Let's
have some queries

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:26.764
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: Yes.

00:11:26.764 --> 00:11:30.004
So we take that one, so
for instance, that just--

00:11:30.004 --> 00:11:30.920
FELIPE HOFFA: Rhianna.

00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:32.211
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: Rhianna.

00:11:32.211 --> 00:11:34.610
So that just takes--
that query table there.

00:11:38.150 --> 00:11:40.290
Find the artists that
are similar to Rhianna

00:11:40.290 --> 00:11:43.650
based on the number of playlists
that have been together.

00:11:43.650 --> 00:11:47.360
So just joining the
playlists together with ID,

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:50.630
and make sure that
the artist that you--

00:11:50.630 --> 00:11:53.560
to find other artists that
have been included in playlists

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:54.544
included Rhianna.

00:11:54.544 --> 00:11:56.210
FELIPE HOFFA: Let's
look at what artists

00:11:56.210 --> 00:11:58.829
are similar to Rhianna based
on appearances on playlists.

00:11:58.829 --> 00:12:00.120
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: Exactly.

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:04.142
So once again,
500,000 playlists.

00:12:04.142 --> 00:12:06.100
Every playlist-- I don't
have the exact number,

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:09.641
but between 10 and 50 tracks.

00:12:09.641 --> 00:12:10.890
And that's what we have there.

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:14.970
I think like Britney Spears,
Beyonce, [INAUDIBLE],

00:12:14.970 --> 00:12:15.970
David Guetta, and so on.

00:12:15.970 --> 00:12:19.024
So that's-- that's also kind
of the way-- if you look--

00:12:19.024 --> 00:12:21.190
I've done something as well,
to use that in compared

00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:25.680
to recommendation they're giving
to existing streaming platform.

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:27.400
And you see as well
there's some overlap.

00:12:27.400 --> 00:12:30.800
So, having the sample is
in a way, relatively small

00:12:30.800 --> 00:12:32.970
compared to the
number of playlists

00:12:32.970 --> 00:12:36.710
that Spotify or Deezer can
have in full-- there are still

00:12:36.710 --> 00:12:37.960
some [INAUDIBLE] there.

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:39.510
FELIPE HOFFA: And in very
few seconds, you can--

00:12:39.510 --> 00:12:40.020
DR. ALEXANDRE
PASSANT: Yes, yeah.

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:41.480
FELIPE HOFFA: You can
start clustering artists.

00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:42.938
DR. ALEXANDRE
PASSANT: It took just

00:12:42.938 --> 00:12:44.760
like six seconds for
500 gigs that's there.

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:45.597
FELIPE HOFFA: Nice.

00:12:45.597 --> 00:12:47.430
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT:
But you can do also--

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:52.000
so that one is artists--
to artist recommendations.

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:53.617
But we can do song
recommendations.

00:12:53.617 --> 00:12:56.200
FELIPE HOFFA: Oh, you can go as
deep as finding similar songs?

00:12:56.200 --> 00:12:57.325
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: Yes.

00:12:57.325 --> 00:13:00.470
So let's say, for instance--
and you can do more than that.

00:13:00.470 --> 00:13:03.330
You can do-- find
similar songs, but if you

00:13:03.330 --> 00:13:06.120
want to make it that more
fun, exclude the songs

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:07.200
from the same artist.

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:11.800
So let's say, that
example there-- we

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:14.350
take "Harder, Faster, Better--"
"Harder, Better, Faster,

00:13:14.350 --> 00:13:15.400
Stronger" from Daft Punk.

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:15.590
FELIPE HOFFA: OK.

00:13:15.590 --> 00:13:17.881
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: And
we find all the other tracks

00:13:17.881 --> 00:13:19.590
that people play
together with that one,

00:13:19.590 --> 00:13:20.950
excluding the Daft Punk tracks.

00:13:20.950 --> 00:13:22.895
So that might be
a way-- and if you

00:13:22.895 --> 00:13:26.160
want to build your own radio
station, you can just use that,

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:28.530
and say well, this
is the track I like.

00:13:28.530 --> 00:13:30.610
Just that track from Daft Punk.

00:13:30.610 --> 00:13:32.730
What you listen to
now but something

00:13:32.730 --> 00:13:34.630
different from Daft Punk.

00:13:34.630 --> 00:13:41.360
So the same kind of query that
instead of finding artists that

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:43.140
are together and
finding tracks that are

00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:44.479
correlated in the same place.

00:13:44.479 --> 00:13:46.020
FELIPE HOFFA: From
different artists?

00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:47.080
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: Yes,
from different artists.

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:48.850
So there I'm
excluding the artist,

00:13:48.850 --> 00:13:52.550
which is that there-- that's
the idea of Daft Punk in Deezer.

00:13:52.550 --> 00:13:54.495
That's the idea of that track.

00:13:54.495 --> 00:13:55.770
So, running the query--

00:13:55.770 --> 00:13:56.520
FELIPE HOFFA: Wow.

00:13:56.520 --> 00:13:57.260
That was fast.

00:13:57.260 --> 00:13:57.844
It was cached.

00:13:57.844 --> 00:13:59.593
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT:
Yes, it was cached.

00:13:59.593 --> 00:14:01.780
But 12 like-- things that
David Guetta, [INAUDIBLE],

00:14:01.780 --> 00:14:02.980
and so on, Bob Sinclair.

00:14:02.980 --> 00:14:04.438
Then there are
things that might be

00:14:04.438 --> 00:14:06.760
not in the same musical genre.

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:10.780
But just you have like Justice
and other French band playing

00:14:10.780 --> 00:14:11.280
as well.

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:12.600
FELIPE HOFFA: People are
grouping them together.

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:14.800
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: Yes
which-- which makes sense

00:14:14.800 --> 00:14:15.930
if you think of it.

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:16.721
FELIPE HOFFA: Cool.

00:14:16.721 --> 00:14:19.240
So from one track, you're
getting more recommendations,

00:14:19.240 --> 00:14:21.420
tracks from other
artists, and looking

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:22.700
at the use of generated data--

00:14:22.700 --> 00:14:22.950
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: Yes.

00:14:22.950 --> 00:14:24.450
FELIPE HOFFA: From
this playlist.

00:14:24.450 --> 00:14:26.630
Um, from our last
sample, I know you

00:14:26.630 --> 00:14:29.360
have worked with streaming
data too with things

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:30.460
that are happening now.

00:14:30.460 --> 00:14:30.950
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: Yes.

00:14:30.950 --> 00:14:31.890
FELIPE HOFFA: Can you
tell me more about that?

00:14:31.890 --> 00:14:33.056
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: Sure.

00:14:33.056 --> 00:14:35.410
So, these two examples
are kind of static,

00:14:35.410 --> 00:14:37.450
in the sense that we
took a bunch of data that

00:14:37.450 --> 00:14:40.400
was done for a static
period of time,

00:14:40.400 --> 00:14:44.390
and pass that into BigQuery
to run the analysis.

00:14:44.390 --> 00:14:46.960
But I've used as well Twitter
and the Twitter stream

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:50.300
to find what are people
sharing in almost real time,

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:54.520
and see from there if we can
find from the Twitter firehose,

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:57.570
at least, from the one we have
from the API, which is subsets.

00:14:57.570 --> 00:15:01.010
But what are the top tracks for
a given genre for the past 24

00:15:01.010 --> 00:15:02.084
hours or 12 hours.

00:15:02.084 --> 00:15:04.250
FELIPE HOFFA: So sounds
that people are sharing now,

00:15:04.250 --> 00:15:04.430
I take it?

00:15:04.430 --> 00:15:05.763
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: Yes, yes.

00:15:05.763 --> 00:15:07.380
And for that, I'm
using the songs

00:15:07.380 --> 00:15:10.690
that people share
using YouTube links.

00:15:10.690 --> 00:15:14.690
And the reason is that YouTube
got this API where you can get

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:16.500
additional data from Frida's.

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:19.800
So that means for every YouTube
link that's shared on Twitter,

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:22.970
I can get additional information
about who is the artist,

00:15:22.970 --> 00:15:25.030
and what is the general
genre of the artist.

00:15:25.030 --> 00:15:29.220
So then you can find--
give me the last pop music

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:32.220
or heavy metal music that
has been shared popularly

00:15:32.220 --> 00:15:33.169
in the past 12 hours.

00:15:33.169 --> 00:15:33.960
FELIPE HOFFA: Nice.

00:15:33.960 --> 00:15:37.720
And you are downloading
getting this data in real time

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:38.530
from Twitter?

00:15:38.530 --> 00:15:39.080
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: Yes.

00:15:39.080 --> 00:15:39.830
FELIPE HOFFA: Yes.

00:15:39.830 --> 00:15:42.470
I want to make a big call
out here for our friends

00:15:42.470 --> 00:15:43.660
at Twitter.

00:15:43.660 --> 00:15:46.280
Ryan Choi at Twitter
developer relations

00:15:46.280 --> 00:15:47.940
has published this
project where you

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:51.350
can collect your own
tweets using Google Compute

00:15:51.350 --> 00:15:53.140
engine as you're doing now.

00:15:53.140 --> 00:15:57.840
And you can run it for
yourself, look at their GitHub

00:15:57.840 --> 00:16:00.250
to find this project.

00:16:00.250 --> 00:16:02.750
So let's see how-- what are
you capturing right now?

00:16:02.750 --> 00:16:04.416
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT:
So I can show you

00:16:04.416 --> 00:16:07.550
for the-- so I have an
instance running right now,

00:16:07.550 --> 00:16:10.507
which gets data
from the firehose.

00:16:10.507 --> 00:16:12.590
FELIPE HOFFA: So this is
how [INAUDIBLE] compute--

00:16:12.590 --> 00:16:13.230
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: Yes.

00:16:13.230 --> 00:16:13.620
FELIPE HOFFA: Compute engine?

00:16:13.620 --> 00:16:15.300
DR. ALEXANDRE
PASSANT: [INAUDIBLE]

00:16:15.300 --> 00:16:18.080
fetching the Twitter stream API.

00:16:24.370 --> 00:16:26.030
So it's fetching right now.

00:16:26.030 --> 00:16:28.120
We have the tweets
coming from the API.

00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:31.400
And each of these 280's
then, so the YouTube link

00:16:31.400 --> 00:16:33.940
is extracted, passed
to the YouTube API

00:16:33.940 --> 00:16:35.229
to get the Frida's IDs.

00:16:35.229 --> 00:16:37.520
FELIPE HOFFA: So these are
Tweets that mention YouTube?

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:39.728
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: Yes--
mentioned YouTube links.

00:16:39.728 --> 00:16:41.740
So I-- the link then,
is sent to the YouTube

00:16:41.740 --> 00:16:44.280
API to get the Frida's IDs.

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:46.760
So that's the entities that
are contained in a video.

00:16:46.760 --> 00:16:50.120
So for a music video,
generally, as the ID back,

00:16:50.120 --> 00:16:52.210
you have the Frida's
ID of the artist,

00:16:52.210 --> 00:16:54.420
and the Frida's ID of the track.

00:16:54.420 --> 00:16:57.250
And then the second step is
to code the Frida's API using

00:16:57.250 --> 00:16:59.660
those IDs, and to get more
information about the artist.

00:16:59.660 --> 00:17:01.670
So [INAUDIBLE]
could have their--

00:17:01.670 --> 00:17:04.460
to get their music and genre.

00:17:04.460 --> 00:17:06.569
So that's all done in real
time, because then one,

00:17:06.569 --> 00:17:08.720
I get that data
back from Frida's.

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:12.839
I'm sending that to BigQuery
using the BigQuery stream API.

00:17:12.839 --> 00:17:16.460
So that means that, like,
the time between the tweets

00:17:16.460 --> 00:17:20.639
is published and the time its
in BigQuery is just a few--

00:17:20.639 --> 00:17:21.930
less than a minute [INAUDIBLE].

00:17:21.930 --> 00:17:24.013
FELIPE HOFFA: So you take
tweet, the YouTube link,

00:17:24.013 --> 00:17:26.010
you have reached this
data, with Frida's data.

00:17:26.010 --> 00:17:27.369
What's the artist?

00:17:27.369 --> 00:17:29.540
What's the genre?

00:17:29.540 --> 00:17:30.040
Nice.

00:17:30.040 --> 00:17:31.892
Can we look at the table here?

00:17:31.892 --> 00:17:33.058
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: Sure.

00:17:35.200 --> 00:17:37.860
FELIPE HOFFA: Let's
leave this out.

00:17:37.860 --> 00:17:41.550
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: So that's
a search of the table there.

00:17:41.550 --> 00:17:46.900
So like in the-- these are use
case passing Gs on objects,

00:17:46.900 --> 00:17:48.250
I'm flattening then to there.

00:17:48.250 --> 00:17:50.022
So we have a tweet
there that contains--

00:17:50.022 --> 00:17:52.230
so that's sort of the
information about the tweet, so

00:17:52.230 --> 00:17:54.400
the question that
user, and so on.

00:17:54.400 --> 00:17:58.550
And then inside the tweet,
there is a YouTube video.

00:17:58.550 --> 00:18:00.090
It will have a
title, a description,

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:02.440
and this video has some topics.

00:18:02.440 --> 00:18:04.350
And every topic gets
an ID and a type.

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:07.180
So the type comes from
Frida's as well as the ID,

00:18:07.180 --> 00:18:10.125
but the type can be music
genre, or music artist.

00:18:10.125 --> 00:18:13.520
And that's how using the
genre, I run the [INAUDIBLE],

00:18:13.520 --> 00:18:14.950
so we can--

00:18:14.950 --> 00:18:17.400
FELIPE HOFFA: What kind of
information are you extracting

00:18:17.400 --> 00:18:20.010
[INAUDIBLE]-- extracting
from this blank data?

00:18:20.010 --> 00:18:21.801
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT:
One thing you can do

00:18:21.801 --> 00:18:24.175
is to-- let's say what we
can-- look at that together.

00:18:24.175 --> 00:18:27.420
But you can even find what
other popular genres that

00:18:27.420 --> 00:18:31.460
are published during
the past 24 or 12 hours,

00:18:31.460 --> 00:18:36.070
and then go deeper and look
just at one particular genre,

00:18:36.070 --> 00:18:38.550
and see what people
post about it.

00:18:38.550 --> 00:18:40.620
So that's let's take
that query there.

00:18:45.150 --> 00:18:47.930
So that query-- and then that's
interesting there because we

00:18:47.930 --> 00:18:51.880
see a combination of Twitter
information and Frida's.

00:18:51.880 --> 00:18:54.160
So we've just seen
in the structure

00:18:54.160 --> 00:18:57.390
that we have some
free base types.

00:18:57.390 --> 00:19:04.220
So there I'm just finding all
types-- all instances of types,

00:19:04.220 --> 00:19:06.985
music genres-- all the
genres-- that have been shared,

00:19:06.985 --> 00:19:08.452
in the past hour--

00:19:08.452 --> 00:19:09.785
FELIPE HOFFA: The last 24 hours.

00:19:09.785 --> 00:19:11.118
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: 24 hours.

00:19:11.118 --> 00:19:14.170
And then for each of them,
finding the number of videos

00:19:14.170 --> 00:19:17.200
and grouping that by
the number of videos.

00:19:17.200 --> 00:19:21.710
That query will give us
the most popular genres

00:19:21.710 --> 00:19:24.886
that have been extracted from
the-- from the data sets.

00:19:24.886 --> 00:19:28.166
FELIPE HOFFA:
Let's look at that.

00:19:28.166 --> 00:19:29.623
Again, [INAUDIBLE] gigabytes?

00:19:29.623 --> 00:19:30.410
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT: Yeah.

00:19:30.410 --> 00:19:31.390
FELIPE HOFFA: Less
than three seconds?

00:19:31.390 --> 00:19:33.410
DR. ALEXANDRE PASSANT:
Less than three seconds.

00:19:33.410 --> 00:19:36.087
So that's the number of
videos there extracted.

00:19:36.087 --> 00:19:37.670
FELIPE HOFFA: We get
the free base ID.

00:19:37.670 --> 00:19:38.180
DR. ALEXANDRE
PASSANT: Yes so that's

00:19:38.180 --> 00:19:40.130
the free base ID of the genre.

00:19:40.130 --> 00:19:43.640
And that's what we have
in our, you know, sample.

00:19:43.640 --> 00:19:46.410
FELIPE HOFFA: Pop music, hip
hop music, rock, acoustic,

00:19:46.410 --> 00:19:47.170
soundtracks.

00:19:47.170 --> 00:19:48.378
